---
/**
 * The opening card: the duck, then the site.
 *
 * Two rules govern this thing, because a loading overlay is the single easiest
 * way to make a working site look broken:
 *
 *   1. It is never shown unless something can also take it down. The class that
 *      reveals it is set by an inline script that also arms a failsafe timer, so
 *      the overlay cannot outlive its own removal. With JavaScript off the class
 *      is never set and this markup stays invisible.
 *
 *   2. It waits for what is actually visible — the hero photograph decoded and
 *      the webfont resolved — rather than the `load` event, which fires long
 *      after both and waits on things nobody is looking at.
 *
 * aria-hidden throughout: this is a curtain, not content. Screen readers get the
 * real page immediately, which is already in the DOM behind it.
 */
---

<div class="loader" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="loader-inner">
    <div class="loader-duck">
      <img src="/assets/duck-square.svg" width="180" height="180" alt="" />
    </div>
    <p class="loader-word">Grey Duck Running</p>
  </div>
</div>
